Subject: [overlay 0046] CL_SyncModeProbe — what CL_Frame's throttle is ACTUALLY doing

Reported in the headset on 1.0.11.24: "why is 60 our max? The M5 Vision Pro can hit 120 Hz but
90 is more common. Can we lock the FPS to whatever refresh rate the Vision Pro is at?"

Answering that needs the client's own frame-throttle state, and there is no way to read it from
outside src/client: `sync_mode`, `main_msec`, `phys_msec`, `ref_msec` and `cls.active` are all
file-static or private, and the shell's VR glue is a separate translation unit compiled against
the PUBLIC headers (inc/), which is why every existing VR instrument had to be added this way.

Three specific things this makes readable, each of which caps the frame rate at a value the
shell cannot see and cannot infer:

  1. `cls.active != ACT_ACTIVATED` -> CL_UpdateFrameTimes picks SYNC_SLEEP_60 ("run at 60 fps
     if not active"), main_msec = 16, and NOTHING the shell sets on cl_maxfps changes it. In a
     merged 2D+3D app whose 2D window is hidden while the immersive space is open, that state
     is one scene-phase callback away at all times. This is a literal "60 is our max" and it
     would look exactly like a renderer problem.
  2. `cls.state != ca_active` does the same thing (loading, demo, disconnected).
  3. cl_async: with async off, `SYNC_MAXFPS` returns from CL_Frame BEFORE prediction and
     CL_UpdateCmd on every throttled tick, so the sim steps at cl_maxfps while the shell
     renders a pair per pose. With async on, phys_msec/ref_msec are separate and the shell can
     set them from the measured layer rate.

Read-only, no behaviour change: one exported function that copies five values and returns the
mode's name from a table of its own (upstream's `sync_names` is USE_DEBUG-only, and an
instrument that compiles out of a release build is not an instrument). Nothing is cached, so a caller always sees the
state CL_Frame will use on its very next tick.

Apply: patch -p1 -d vendor/q2repro < this
--- a/src/client/main.c
+++ b/src/client/main.c
@@ -3260,6 +3260,36 @@
 
     Com_DDPrintf("%s: mode=%s main_msec=%d ref_msec=%d, phys_msec=%d\n",
                  __func__, sync_names[sync_mode], main_msec, ref_msec, phys_msec);
+}
+
+// [vr overlay 0046] The frame-throttle state, for a caller outside src/client (the visionOS
+// VR shell's pacing controller). Every value here is file-static or private to this
+// translation unit, and all five together are what decides how often the sim actually steps.
+// Read-only and uncached: the caller sees exactly what the next CL_Frame will use.
+//
+// The mode's name comes from a table of its own rather than `sync_names`, which only exists
+// under USE_DEBUG — an instrument that compiles out of a release build is not an instrument.
+//
+// Returns the sync mode's name (never NULL). Any out pointer may be NULL.
+const char *CL_SyncModeProbe(int *active, int *state, int *main_ms, int *phys_ms, int *ref_ms)
+{
+    static const char *const names[] = {
+        "SYNC_TIMEDEMO", "SYNC_MAXFPS", "SYNC_SLEEP_10",
+        "SYNC_SLEEP_60", "ASYNC_VIDEO", "ASYNC_FULL"
+    };
+    if (active)
+        *active = cls.active;
+    if (state)
+        *state = cls.state;
+    if (main_ms)
+        *main_ms = main_msec;
+    if (phys_ms)
+        *phys_ms = phys_msec;
+    if (ref_ms)
+        *ref_ms = ref_msec;
+    if ((unsigned)sync_mode >= q_countof(names))
+        return "SYNC_UNKNOWN";
+    return names[sync_mode];
 }
 
 void CL_AddHitMarker(void)
